ng flowchart and algorithm, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Computer Programming task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** History of C is addressed within the syllabus unit “Fundamentals of C”. A complete answer should define the term precisely, explain its role in the unit, identify its main inputs or components, and describe how it is applied or evaluated in a realistic task. Relate it to nearby syllabus concepts such as Structure of C program, compilation and execution, Compiler and Interpreter, C tokens, character set, then give a specific example and state a limitation or verification step.\n\n**Principles or working (3 marks):** Explain the main elements in a logical order. In this syllabus unit, the closely related areas are Structure of C program; compilation and execution; Compiler and Interpreter; C tokens. Relate each element to the purpose of History of C, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Computer Programming task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** A conditional statement selects a path based on a Boolean expression. The condition should cover the intended cases, and mutually exclusive or ordered conditions must be structured to avoid unreachable branches.\n\n**Principles or working (3 marks):** Explain the main elements in a logical order. In this syllabus unit, the closely related areas are simple If statement; If-else; nested If- else; else-if ladder. Relate each element to the purpose of Decision making statements, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Computer Programming task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** An array stores a sequence of elements accessible by index. Index bounds, element type and traversal order matter; an out-of-range access is invalid in C and can cause undefined behaviour.\n\n**Principles or working (3 marks):** Explain the main elements in a logical order. In this syllabus unit, the closely related areas are declaration & initialization of 1-D array; operation in array; String- declaration & initialization; string functions. Relate each element to the purpose of Types of arrays, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Computer Programming task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

