VALUES (4400913121020125, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Algebra of Matrices in the context of Determinants and Matrices. Define it and describe its key principles or steps, with one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain System of Linear Equation in the context of Equations. Define it and describe its key principles or steps, with one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Introduction of Function in the context of Function. Define it and describe its key principles or steps, with one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Definition and some properties of limits in the context of Limit of functions. Define it and describe its key principles or steps, with one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** The determinant is a scalar associated with a square matrix. A non-zero determinant means the matrix is invertible; determinants also help solve systems of linear equations.\n\n**Principles or working (3 marks):** Explain the main elements in a logical order. In this syllabus unit, the closely related areas are Determinant of Matrix; Inverse of Matrix up to 3×3 matrix; Solution of Simultaneous Equation (up to 3 variable); Determinants and Matrices. Relate each element to the purpose of Algebra of Matrices, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Mathematics-II task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** System of Linear Equation is addressed within the syllabus unit “Equations”. A complete answer should define the term precisely, explain its role in the unit, identify its main inputs or components, and describe how it is applied or evaluated in a realistic task. Relate it to nearby syllabus concepts such as Solution of Quadratic Equation, Solution of Cubic Equation, Equations, then give a specific example and state a limitation or verification step.\n\n**Principles or working (3 marks):** Explain the main elements in a logical order. In this syllabus unit, the closely related areas are Solution of Quadratic Equation; Solution of Cubic Equation; Equations. Relate each element to the purpose of System of Linear Equation, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Mathematics-II task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** A function groups a named operation into a reusable unit. Parameters provide input, a return value can provide output, and a clear interface reduces duplication and helps divide a program into testable components.\n\n**Principles or working (3 marks):** Explain the main elements in a logical order. In this syllabus unit, the closely related areas are Algebra of Function; Example of Function; Function. Relate each element to the purpose of Introduction of Function, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Mathematics-II task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** A function groups a named operation into a reusable unit. Parameters provide input, a return value can provide output, and a clear interface reduces duplication and helps divide a program into testable components.\n\n**Principles or working (3 marks):** Explain the main elements in a logical order. In this syllabus unit, the closely related areas are Limit of polynomial and rational functions; Limit of trigonometric function; Limit of exponential function; Limit at infinity. Relate each element to the purpose of Definition and some properties of limits, and state any conditions or assumptions that affect the result.\n\n**Application and example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a realistic Mathematics-II task. Identify the requirement, select the relevant procedure or component, and explain the expected result instead of only listing terminology.\n\n**Check and conclusion (1 mark):** Verify the outcome against the stated requirement; mention a meaningful limitation, error source or design trade-off. End by stating why the concept is suitable for the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; accurate principles or steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

